WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.118
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:15.716
Unit twenty: Philosophy of Language. Today you will explain meaning using abstract noun clauses.

4
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.225
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:24.887
Meaning.

7
00:00:25.883 --> 00:00:29.044
Meaning means what a word, sentence or action conveys.

8
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.091
The meaning of 'bank' changes with context.

9
00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:34.674
Your turn. Say: Meaning.

10
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:38.349
Meaning.

11
00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:40.201
Reference.

12
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:44.644
Reference means the relation between a word and the thing it picks out.

13
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.422
Frege distinguished the reference of a name from its sense.

14
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:51.913
Your turn. Say: Reference.

15
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.023
Reference.

16
00:00:56.783 --> 00:00:57.610
Truth.

17
00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:02.439
Truth means the property of a statement that corresponds to how things are.

18
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:06.176
Truth conditions specify when a sentence would be true.

19
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.343
Your turn. Say: Truth.

20
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:12.899
Truth.

21
00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:14.883
Speech act.

22
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:19.810
Speech act means an utterance that performs an action, such as promising.

23
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:22.973
'I promise' is a speech act, not a description.

24
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:26.108
Your turn. Say: Speech act.

25
00:01:28.967 --> 00:01:30.095
Speech act.

26
00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.990
Context.

27
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.330
Context means the circumstances in which something is said.

28
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:40.553
Context determines whether 'It's cold' is a complaint or a request.

29
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:43.721
Your turn. Say: Context.

30
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:47.510
Context.

31
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:49.437
Ambiguity.

32
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.214
Ambiguity means the property of having more than one possible meaning.

33
00:01:54.817 --> 00:01:57.373
The headline's ambiguity caused confusion.

34
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:00.415
Your turn. Say: Ambiguity.

35
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:04.419
Ambiguity.

36
00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:06.405
Pragmatics.

37
00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:10.647
Pragmatics means the study of how context shapes meaning.

38
00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.040
Pragmatics explains why 'Nice weather!' can be sarcastic.

39
00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.569
Your turn. Say: Pragmatics.

40
00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:22.713
Pragmatics.

41
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:24.730
Interpretation.

42
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:30.073
Interpretation means the act of explaining or understanding the meaning of something.

43
00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:33.925
Legal interpretation depends on text and context.

44
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:37.401
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

45
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:41.500
Interpretation.

46
00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.962
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:51.875
Visal, I said 'Can you open the window?' and he just said 'Yes.' Was he being rude?

48
00:02:52.983 --> 00:03:01.928
Literally he was correct. What a word means depends on how it is used, and what a sentence means depends on who says it and why.

49
00:03:03.017 --> 00:03:05.225
So the real meaning was a request?

50
00:03:06.317 --> 00:03:16.736
Yes, a speech act. Whether an utterance is a question or a request is determined by context, not by its grammar alone.

51
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.432
Then meaning is never fixed?

52
00:03:20.517 --> 00:03:28.614
That is what Wittgenstein suggested. Why a phrase works is explained by the practice around it, not by any dictionary.

53
00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:31.511
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:35.104
Grammar focus: Abstract noun clauses.

55
00:03:35.404 --> 00:03:36.603
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:39.943
What a word means depends on how it is used.

57
00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:44.359
Whether an utterance is a request is determined by its context.

58
00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.527
Why a phrase works is explained by the practice around it.

59
00:03:49.467 --> 00:03:58.342
A wh- clause keeps statement order: What a word means (not 'What does a word mean'). The clause is singular and takes a singular verb: depends, is.

60
00:03:59.083 --> 00:04:03.442
Be careful! Don't say: What means a word depends on use.

61
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.154
Say: What a word means depends on use.

62
00:04:08.454 --> 00:04:11.255
Noun clauses keep statement word order.

63
00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:14.963
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.035
Words and Their Work.

65
00:04:17.335 --> 00:04:22.875
The philosophy of language asks how words come to mean anything and how speakers succeed in communicating.

66
00:04:23.867 --> 00:04:30.615
Frege separated the reference of an expression, the object it picks out, from its sense, the way in which that object is presented.

67
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:37.570
Wittgenstein later argued that meaning is use: what a word means depends on how it functions within a form of life.

68
00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:46.736
J. L. Austin showed that many utterances, such as promises and apologies, perform actions instead of merely describing them.

69
00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:54.516
Pragmatics extends the point: how an utterance should be interpreted depends on context, intention and shared background.

70
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.811
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.520
blank a word means depends on how it is used.

72
00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.439
A, What. B, Which does. C, That.

73
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.570
The answer is A: What.

74
00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:16.541
An utterance that performs an action is a blank.

75
00:05:16.841 --> 00:05:20.831
A, reference. B, speech act. C, truth.

76
00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.610
The answer is B: speech act.

77
00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.597
The study of how context shapes meaning is blank.

78
00:05:30.897 --> 00:05:34.957
A, pragmatics. B, ambiguity. C, reference.

79
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.662
The answer is A: pragmatics.

80
00:05:41.817 --> 00:05:43.445
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.745 --> 00:05:52.181
A, What does a word mean depends on use.. B, What a word means depends on use.. C, What a word does mean depends on use..

82
00:05:55.733 --> 00:05:59.091
The answer is B: What a word means depends on use..

83
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:03.654
According to the reading, Wittgenstein argued that meaning is…

84
00:06:03.954 --> 00:06:08.513
A, use. B, fixed by dictionaries. C, private.

85
00:06:12.067 --> 00:06:13.695
The answer is A: use.

86
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.005
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.871
Can a sentence mean different things in different contexts?

88
00:06:38.683 --> 00:06:40.149
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:40.449 --> 00:07:06.875
Certainly. 'It's cold in here' may be a simple report, a complaint or a polite request to close the window. What it means depends on who says it, to whom and why. Context fills the gap between the words and the speaker's intention. That is why interpretation is not a mechanical decoding of grammar but a reasoned judgment about purpose.

90
00:07:07.175 --> 00:07:08.745
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:14.009
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and abstract noun clauses.

92
00:07:14.309 --> 00:07:19.539
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:22.793
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.093 --> 00:07:26.395
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

